'''Taylor Rehmet''' (born July 17, 1992)<ref name=":0">{{cite web|last=Goodman|first=J. David|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/01/29/us/politics/taylor-rehmet-leigh-wambsganss-texas-election.html|title=A Tight Statehouse Race in Texas Offers Republicans a Warning|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=January 29, 2026|accessdate=February 1, 2026|quote=He grew up Republican, he said, because that’s what both his parents were. After joining his union and seeing the benefits he got as a young worker, he began to shift politically because he saw Democrats as being more supportive of organized labor.}}</ref> is an American politician, union leader, and aircraft mechanic elected in 2026 to represent the [[Texas's 9th Senate district|9th district]] in the [[Texas Senate]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], Rehmet gained national attention after he won the runoff election held on January 31, 2026, by 14.4%.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Jankowski |first=Philip |date=January 31, 2026 |title=Democrat Taylor Rehmet shocks in upset win for conservative Tarrant County Senate seat |url=https://www.dallasnews.com/news/elections/2026/01/31/early-returns-show-democrat-taylor-rehmet-with-lead-in-tarrant-county-senate-runoff/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260201014903/https://www.dallasnews.com/news/elections/2026/01/31/early-returns-show-democrat-taylor-rehmet-with-lead-in-tarrant-county-senate-runoff/ |archive-date=February 1, 2026 |work=[[The Dallas Morning News]]}}</ref> The district, which Republican [[Donald Trump]] won by 17 points [[2024 United States presidential election in Texas#General election|in 2024]],<ref name="Tsiaperas">{{cite news |last=Tsiaperas |first=Tasha |title=Democrat leads in early votes for Tarrant County state Senate seat |url=https://www.axios.com/local/dallas/2026/02/01/taylor-rehmet-leigh-wambsganss-texas-senate |access-date=January 31, 2026 |work=[[Axios (website)|Axios]] |date=January 31, 2026}}</ref> had not been represented by a Democrat since 1991.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Shaw |first=Drew |date=2026-01-15 |title=A 'bellwether' election: Tarrant's Texas Senate runoff draws national attention |url=https://fortworthreport.org/2026/01/14/a-bellwether-election-tarrants-texas-senate-runoff-draws-national-attention/ |access-date=2026-02-02 |work=[[Fort Worth Report]] |language=en-US |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260201030846/https://fortworthreport.org/2026/01/14/a-bellwether-election-tarrants-texas-senate-runoff-draws-national-attention/ |archive-date=February 1, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

== Early life and career ==
Rehmet was born in [[Garland, Texas]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Downen |first=Robert |date=2026-01-23 |title=In Tarrant County, a Hub for the Far Right, a Democrat Seeks a Major Upset |url=https://www.texasmonthly.com/news-politics/tarrant-county-senate-race-taylor-rehmet-seeks-upset/ |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20260124071535/https://www.texasmonthly.com/news-politics/tarrant-county-senate-race-taylor-rehmet-seeks-upset/ |archive-date=2026-01-24 |access-date=2026-02-01 |work=Texas Monthly |quote=Rehmet, a gregarious, dark-blond 33-year-old, was born in Garland, just northeast of Dallas, raised by an airline mechanic and a salon worker. He joined the Air Force at nineteen and served four years on active duty before moving to Fort Worth for a job at Lockheed Martin, where he is also an aircraft mechanic.}}</ref> His father worked as an airline mechanic and his mother worked in a salon.<ref name=":1" /> Rehmet stated that he grew up [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]].<ref name=":0" /> At the age of 19, he joined the [[United States Air Force]] and served as an electrical and environmental systems specialist in [[Minot, North Dakota]].<ref name=":1" /><ref name="WhoIS">{{cite news|url=https://www.star-telegram.com/news/politics-government/election/article314532031.html|title=Who is Taylor Rehmet, the Fort Worth Democrat who flipped a TX state Senate seat?|first=Eleanor|last=Dearman|date=February 1, 2026|access-date=February 1, 2026|work=[[The Fort Worth Star-Telegram]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260202093525/https://www.star-telegram.com/news/politics-government/election/article314532031.html |archive-date=February 2, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

After four years of active duty, he left the Air Force and joined [[Lockheed Martin]] as an aircraft mechanic in [[Fort Worth, Texas]], in 2017.<ref name="WhoIS"/> He serves as president of his local and state chapter of the [[International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers]].<ref name=":1" />

== Texas Senate==
After Republican [[Kelly Hancock]] resigned from the [[Texas Senate]] to become the acting [[Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts|Texas comptroller]], Rehmet ran for the vacant seat to fill his term ending January 2027. In the November 5, 2025, special election, Rehmet placed first with 47.6% of the vote ahead of [[GOP|Republicans]] [[Leigh Wambsganss]] and John Huffman.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.star-telegram.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article312730646.html|title=Taylor Rehmet, Leigh Wambsganss headed to runoff in Senate District 9|first=Eleanor|last=Dearman|date=November 5, 2025|access-date=February 1, 2026|work=[[The Fort Worth Star-Telegram]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260202094137/https://www.star-telegram.com/news/politics-government/state-politics/article312730646.html |archive-date=February 2, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Rehmet and Wambsganss advanced to a runoff on January 31, 2026, where Rehmet won by over 14% of the vote.<ref name="Tsiaperas"/>

Media noted that the district was reliably Republican and that [[Donald Trump]] had won the district by 17 points in the [[2024 United States presidential election in Texas|2024 presidential election]], making Rehmet's victory an [[political upset|upset]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/01/us/democratic-upset-texas-district-republicans.html|title=Democratic Upset in Deep Red Texas District Rattles Republicans|date=February 1, 2026|access-date=February 1, 2026|work=[[The New York Times]]|first=J. David |last=Goodman}}</ref> Going into the January run-off election he spent [[US$]]70,000, and Wambsganss reported $736,000 in expenditure. He received $500,000 in [[independent expenditures]] from [[VoteVets]] and $143,383 in donations from the [[Texas Democratic Party]]'s Texas Majority PAC.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.texastribune.org/2026/01/30/texas-senate-district-9-runoff-rehmet-wambsganss-special-election/|first=Alejandro|last=Serrano|date=January 30, 2026|access-date=February 1, 2026|title=Democrat Taylor Rehmet wins solidly red Texas Senate seat in stunning special election upset|work=[[The Texas Tribune]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260131012357/https://www.texastribune.org/2026/01/30/texas-senate-district-9-runoff-rehmet-wambsganss-special-election/ |archive-date=January 31, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

He was sworn into office on February 19, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jankowski |first=Philip |date=February 19, 2026 |title=Texas Democrat who shocked Republican establishment sworn in as senator Thursday |url=https://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/2026/02/19/democrat-who-shocked-republican-establishment-to-be-sworn-in-as-senator-thursday/ |website=Dallas News |language=en}}</ref>

