'''Rainelle Sara Krause''' (December 14, 1987 – March 16, 2026)<ref name=":0">{{cite web |last1=Krause |first1=Rainelle |title=Home |url=https://www.rainellekrause.com/ |website=Rainelle Krause |access-date=March 26, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> was an American opera singer and [[Acrobatics|aerialist]], known for her combination of [[coloratura soprano]] and [[Aerial silk|aerial silks]] performance. Her signature piece was the [[Queen of the Night aria]], "Der Hölle Rache kocht in meinem Herzen", from Mozart's ''[[The Magic Flute]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Brown |first=Steven |date=February 5, 2022 |title=Singers provide moments of vocal artistry in HGO's machine-like "Magic Flute" |url=https://texasclassicalreview.com/2022/02/05/singers-provide-moments-of-vocal-artistry-in-hgos-machine-like-magic-flute/ |access-date=March 18, 2026 |website=Texas Classical Review}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Paget |first=Clive |date=February 29, 2024 |title=''The Magic Flute'' review – ENO's revival is a peach of wit, wisdom and laughs |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2024/feb/29/the-magic-flute-review-mozart-mcburney-eno-revival-is-a-peach-of-wit-wisdom-and-laughs |access-date=March 18, 2026 |newspaper=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref>

== Early life and career ==
Krause was born in [[Tampa, Florida]],<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |title=Krause, Rainelle |url=https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2012033813.html |access-date=April 9, 2026 |website=Library of Congress Authorities}}</ref> on December 14, 1987,<ref name=":0" /> and grew up in [[Bloomington, Indiana]]. She received her [[Bachelor of Music]] and [[Master of Music]] in vocal performance at the [[Jacobs School of Music]] at [[Indiana University]] in Bloomington.<ref>{{Cite podcast |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5H6HcDXcnLY |title=Defying gravity with stratospheric coloratura soprano Rainelle Krause |website=Between the Notes |last=Hymel |first=Bryan |date=February 20, 2026 |access-date=March 18, 2026 |last2=Hurt |first2=Weston |archive-date=March 22, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260322082631/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5H6HcDXcnLY |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Rainelle Krause: Soprano|url=https://www.eno.org/people/rainelle-krause/ |access-date=March 19, 2026 |publisher=[[English National Opera]]}}</ref>

After graduating, she travelled to Europe and performed in opera houses such as the [[English National Opera]], the [[Berlin State Opera]], the [[Dutch National Opera]], the [[Royal Danish Theatre]], and [[Theater Basel]], among many others.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=March 18, 2026 |title=Met Opera Soprano Rainelle Krause dies suddenly at 37, opera world mourns |url=https://www.geo.tv/latest/656256-met-opera-soprano-rainelle-krause-dies-suddenly-at-37-opera-world-mourns |access-date=March 19, 2026 |website=[[Geo News]]}}</ref>

In July 2020, Krause's singing the "Queen of the Night aria" while practicing [[acrobatics]] and [[Aerial silk|aerial silks]] went viral.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 22, 2020 |title=Incredible soprano sings 'Queen of the Night' aria while upside down on aerial silks |url=https://www.classicfm.com/music-news/videos/soprano-queen-of-the-night-aerial-silks/ |access-date=March 18, 2026 |website=[[Classic FM (UK)|Classic FM]]}}</ref> In April 2025, at the Nashville Opera, she performed as Lucia in [[Gaetano Donizetti|Donizetti's]] opera ''[[Lucia di Lammermoor]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stumpfl |first=Amy |date=April 1, 2025 |title=Rainelle Krause Prepares for ''Lucia'' |url=https://www.nashvillescene.com/music/features/rainelle-krause-interview-nashville-opera-lucia/article_d7c44216-05de-4220-a8ec-c8326ad90590.html |access-date=March 18, 2026 |website=[[Nashville Scene]] |archive-date=July 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250724041650/https://www.nashvillescene.com/music/features/rainelle-krause-interview-nashville-opera-lucia/article_d7c44216-05de-4220-a8ec-c8326ad90590.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Later that year, in September, she was recorded and filmed singing the "Queen of the Night aria" at the [[English National Opera]] during the one-off performance, ''Mozart's Women: A Musical Journey'', for the television channel [[Sky Arts]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Jeal |first=Erica |date=September 15, 2025 |title=Mozart's Women: A Musical Journey review – Lauren Laverne helms an insight-free night that goes out with a bang |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2025/sep/15/mozarts-women-lauren-laverne-coliseum-london |access-date=March 19, 2026|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web<!-- |author=Staff Writer --> |date=July 2, 2025 |title=Mozart's Women: A Musical Journey Announces Star-Studded Cast for One-Night-Only Concert at London Coliseum |url=https://theatreweekly.com/mozarts-women-a-musical-journey-announces-star-studded-cast-for-one-night-only-concert-at-london-coliseum/ |access-date=March 19, 2026 |website=Theatre Weekly}}</ref> By October 2025, Krause had performed the role of the Queen of the Night in more than 145 performances of ''The Magic Flute''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=MacKie |first=Alexa |date=October 15, 2025 |title=''The Magic Flute'' almost sunk Opera Atelier. Now, it's back |url=https://www.thestar.com/entertainment/stage/the-magic-flute-nearly-tanked-opera-atelier-three-decades-later-its-one-of-their-signature/article_4073d4b8-cd75-4429-8573-0df8f6886312.html |access-date=March 19, 2026 |work=[[Toronto Star]]}}</ref> In December 2025, she made her [[Metropolitan Opera]] debut, again singing the Queen of the Night.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Salazar |first=Francisco |date=August 7, 2025 |title=Q & A: Rainelle Krause on the Queen of the Night & Singing It in Her Six Productions for the 2025–26 Season |url=https://operawire.com/q-a-rainelle-krause-on-the-queen-of-the-night-singing-it-in-her-six-productions-for-the-2025-26-season/ |access-date=March 18, 2026 |website=OperaWire}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Barone |first=Joshua |date=December 23, 2025 |title=Miracle on 64th Street: Options for Holiday Opera |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/12/23/arts/music/amahl-and-the-night-visitors-the-magic-flute-review.html |access-date=March 18, 2026 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
In 2010, she married Ryan Krause.<ref name=":2" />

Rainelle Krause died on March 16, 2026, after a short hospitalization in [[Fort Worth, Texas]], at the age of 38. Prior to her death, she had been scheduled to perform ''The Magic Flute'' at the [[Santa Fe Opera]] in the summer of 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Davis |first=Hazel |date=March 18, 2026 |title=Viral 'Queen of the Night' soprano who just made her Met Opera debut dies aged 37 |url=https://www.classicfm.com/music-news/queen-of-the-night-soprano-rainelle-krause-has-died/ |access-date=March 18, 2026 |website=[[Classic FM (UK)|Classic FM]] |archive-date=March 19, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260319114918/https://www.classicfm.com/music-news/queen-of-the-night-soprano-rainelle-krause-has-died/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Salazar |first=Francisco |date=March 17, 2026 |title=Obituary: Soprano Rainelle Krause Passes Away |url=https://operawire.com/obituary-soprano-rainelle-krause-passes-away/ |access-date=March 18, 2026 |website=OperaWire}}</ref>

