'''Emily Gregory''' (born May 11, 1985<ref name="questionnaire">{{cite news |last1=Gregory |first1=Emily |title=Questionnaire: Emily Gregory, Democratic candidate for House District 87 |url=https://www.sun-sentinel.com/2025/12/24/questionnaire-emily-gregory-democratic-candidate-for-house-district-87/ |access-date=March 26, 2026 |work=[[Sun Sentinel]] |date=December 24, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251224210616/https://www.sun-sentinel.com/2025/12/24/questionnaire-emily-gregory-democratic-candidate-for-house-district-87/ |archive-date=December 24, 2025 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription}}</ref>) is an American  [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] politician serving as a member of the [[Florida House of Representatives]] representing the [[Florida's 87th House of Representatives district|87th district]].<ref name="ballotpedia">{{Cite web |title=Emily Gregory |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Emily_Gregory |access-date=March 25, 2026 |website=[[Ballotpedia]] |language=en}}</ref> She won a [[2026 United States state legislative elections#Florida 2|2026 special election]] which was notable as her district is home to [[Mar-a-Lago]], [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[President of the United States|President]] [[Donald Trump]]'s personal residence and voted for Trump in the [[2024 United States presidential election]] by 11 points.<ref>{{cite news |last1=English |first1=Molly |last2=Kaye |first2=Randi |title=Democrat Emily Gregory will flip deep-red Florida House district that includes Mar-a-Lago, CNN projects {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/24/politics/florida-democrats-state-district-mar-a-lago-special-election |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260324112157/https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/24/politics/florida-democrats-state-district-mar-a-lago-special-election |archive-date=March 24, 2026 |work=[[CNN]] |date=March 24, 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Jonathan J. |last=Cooper |title=Democrat flips seat on special election for Florida District that includes Trump's Mar-a-lago resort |url=https://apnews.com/article/democrat-emily-gregory-florida-legislative-seat-maralago-899016be8e87645f7776fa0cca94e1bc |access-date=March 25, 2026 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=March 24, 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

== Education ==
<!-- Per [[MOS:FIELD]]: The names of fields of study are not capitalized unless they are proper nouns. For example, "a bachelors' in English literature" and "Bachelor of Arts in English literature" versus "a bachelor's in political science." -->Gregory has a [[Bachelor of Science]] in health and exercise science from [[Wake Forest University]] and a [[Professional degrees of public health|Master of Public Health]] in health policy and management from [[Columbia University]].<ref name="economictimes">{{cite news |last1=Singh |first1=Divyadeep |title=Who is Emily Gregory? Democrat raises alarm bells for Trump, flips seat in special election for Florida district including Trump's Mar-a-Lago resort |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/us/news/who-is-emily-gregory-democrat-raises-alarm-bells-for-trump-flips-seat-in-special-election-for-florida-district-including-trumps-mar-a-lago-resort/articleshow/129789617.cms |access-date=March 25, 2026 |work=[[The Economic Times]] |date=March 25, 2026}}</ref>

== Florida House of Representatives ==
Gregory defeated Republican Jon Maples, who was endorsed by President Donald Trump, who carried the district by 19 points in the 2024 presidential election. Her first-ever political campaign, Gregory won the special election with a 2.4 point margin after the seat was vacated by Republican [[Mike Caruso (politician)|Mike Caruso]] after he became the [[Palm Beach County, Florida|Palm Beach County]] clerk and comptroller.<ref name=":0" /> Much of her campaign centered on the [[cost-of-living crisis]] and family issues.<ref name="Scott2026">{{cite news |last1=Scott |first1=Owen |title=Emily Gregory: The ‘FIT4MOM’ first-timer who flipped Republican seat in Trump’s backyard |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/emily-gregory-trump-florida-election-b2945250.html |work=[[The Independent]] |date=March 26, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325163749/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/emily-gregory-trump-florida-election-b2945250.html |archive-date=March 25, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In October 2025, Gregory and the [[American Civil Liberties Union|ACLU]] sued Republican Governor [[Ron DeSantis]] alleging violation of Florida law for failing to call a special election to fill the seat.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Grimes |first=Jim |date=October 7, 2025 |title=Candidate files lawsuit to force Gov. DeSantis to call special election in Palm Beach Co. |url=https://cbs12.com/news/local/candidate-files-lawsuit-to-force-governor-ron-desantis-to-call-special-election-in-palm-beach-county-palm-beach-county-circuit-court-by-emily-gregory-october-7-2025 |access-date=May 10, 2026 |work=[[WPEC]] |language=en}}</ref> The seat had been vacated by Caruso on August 18, 2025, resulting in the 87th district not having representation in the Florida House of Representatives for over seven months including during its 2026 regular session.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Perry |first1=Mitch |title=Legislative elections to (finally) take place in three Florida counties next Tuesday |url=https://floridaphoenix.com/2026/03/17/legislative-elections-to-finally-take-place-in-three-florida-counties-on-tuesday/ |access-date=May 27, 2026 |work=[[Florida Phoenix]] |date=March 17, 2026}}</ref>

She was sworn into office on April 7, 2026, at the [[Palm Beach Gardens, Florida|Palm Beach Gardens]] City Hall.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hait |first=Ari |date=April 8, 2026 |title=Emily Gregory sworn in as Florida House District 87 representative |url=https://www.wpbf.com/article/florida-emily-gregory-sworn-in-as-florida-house-district-87-representative/70956885 |access-date=April 8, 2026 |work=[[WPBF]] |language=en}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Gregory is a [[Catholic]].<ref name="economictimes" /> Gregory's husband, Andrew Gregory, is a lieutenant colonel in the [[United States Army]]. They have three children.<ref>{{cite news |editor-last1=Verma |editor-first1=Jatin |title=Who is Emily Gregory? Democrat who flipped Trump's Mar-a-Lago district in Florida |url=https://www.wionews.com/world/who-is-emily-gregory-democrat-who-flipped-trump-s-mar-a-lago-district-in-florida-1774405523796 |access-date=March 25, 2026 |work=[[WION]] |date=March 25, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> Gregory previously owned a small business, running a fitness community for pregnant and [[Postpartum period|postpartum]] women.<ref name=":0">{{cite news |last1=Ventura |first1=Tiago |title=Meet Emily Gregory, the Democrat Who Flipped Trump's Mar-a-Lago District |work=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |date=March 25, 2026 |url=https://time.com/article/2026/03/25/emily-gregory-mar-a-lago-trump-district-florida-state-house/ |archive-date=March 25, 2026 |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325193812/https://time.com/article/2026/03/25/emily-gregory-mar-a-lago-trump-district-florida-state-house/}}</ref>

